=================================================================

This application itself is MIT-licensed.

Tablet-side relay: `resources/android/arm64-v8a/udp2tcp`
--------------------------------------------------------
Our own program, built from `udp2tcp.c` in this repository with the Android NDK
(`aarch64-linux-android26-clang`).  Statically linked against nothing but
Bionic; it is pushed to `/data/local/tmp` at run time and removed by the app's
cleanup.  No Google code is included in it.

adb / platform-tools: bundled, and Google's own licence applies to it
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The app ships the one dependency it cannot work without, so no machine is ever
asked to install platform-tools and the first launch after a login — where the
network is usually not up yet — needs no download:

    resources/platform-tools/linux/adb
    resources/platform-tools/darwin/adb
    resources/platform-tools/win32/adb.exe   (+ the AdbWinApi / AdbWinUsbApi DLLs)

`adb` and those DLLs are Google's, from
`https://dl.google.com/android/repository/platform-tools-latest-<platform>.zip`,
and the copy in the package is fetched at **package time** by `bin/cli.mjs
--fetch-adb` (which `npm run dist` runs first).  The repository does not vendor
the binaries — `.gitignore` excludes `app/resources/platform-tools/` — so a
source checkout has none, and anyone redistributing a build of this app carries
Google's terms with it: the Android SDK Terms of Service / the Android Software
Development Kit License Agreement.  The `NOTICE.txt` Google ships inside
platform-tools is copied next to each `adb` as the attribution it asks for.

The app still prefers an adb that is already on the machine — `$ADB`, then
`$PATH`, then the standard Android SDK locations, and only then its own copy (see
`src/main/platform.ts`) — and it never installs anything system-wide, never
edits `$PATH`, and never asks for admin for adb.  The run-time download of the
same archive remains as the last resort: a build whose copy was stripped, or a
machine where the bundled binary will not start, still ends with a working link.
That copy lives in the app's own data directory, and deleting the directory
removes it completely.

Electron, TypeScript, electron-builder: build-time dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------
Declared as `devDependencies` in `package.json`; Electron's own notices ship with
whatever package electron-builder produces.  There are no third-party runtime
dependencies: the ZIP reader (`src/main/zip.ts`), the relay, and the CLI are all
plain Node standard library.

